### Quick answer  
**Horizontal centering of inline text** → use the `text‑align` property on the element that contains the text.

```css
/* Center all text inside a <p>, <div>, <section>, etc. */
.centered {
    text-align: center;
}
```

```html
<div class="centered">
    <p>This paragraph will be centered.</p>
</div>
```

That’s the most common way to center plain text.  
If you need to center **the whole element** (e.g., a block‑level box) or you want **vertical centering**, you’ll usually use Flexbox or Grid.

---

## 1. Horizontal centering with `text-align`

| Situation | CSS | Why it works |
|-----------|------|--------------|
| Centering text inside a block element (e.g., `<div>`, `<section>`) | `text-align: center;` | The property aligns **inline content** (text, inline‑block, inline‑flex) inside the block container. |
| Centering a single line of text inside a button or link | Same as above, just apply to the button/link. | Buttons are inline‑block by default, so they inherit the same rule. |

**Tip:** `text-align` does **not** affect block‑level children (e.g., another `<div>`). It only aligns the *inline* content inside the container.

---

## 2. Centering a block element horizontally (margin auto)

If you want to center a block‑level box itself (e.g., a card, an image, a form), give it a fixed width and set its horizontal margins to `auto`.

```css
.box {
    width: 300px;          /* any width you need */
    margin: 0 auto;       /* top/bottom = 0, left/right = auto → centered */
}
```

```html
<div class="box">
    <p>This whole box is centered on the page.</p>
</div>
```

---

## 3. Vertical (and horizontal) centering with Flexbox

Flexbox is the go‑to solution when you need both axes centered, or when the content height is dynamic.

```css
.flex-center {
    display: flex;               /* activate flex layout */
    justify-content: center;     /* horizontal alignment */
    align-items: center;         /* vertical alignment */
    /* optional: fill the whole viewport */
    height: 100vh;               /* 100% of the viewport height */
}
```

```html
<div class="flex-center">
    <p>Both horizontally **and** vertically centered!</p>
</div>
```

* `justify-content: center` aligns items along the **main axis** (default is horizontal).  
* `align-items: center` aligns items along the **cross axis** (default is vertical).

---

## 4. Vertical centering with CSS Grid

Grid works similarly to Flexbox but can be more intuitive if you already use a grid layout.

```css
.grid-center {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;   /* shorthand for justify-items + align-items */
    height: 100vh;
}
```

```html
<div class="grid-center">
    <p>Centered with CSS Grid.</p>
</div>
```

---

## 5. One‑liner for inline‑block elements (e.g., an image)

If you have an inline‑block element like an `<img>` and you want it centered inside a container that uses `text-align`, you can simply combine the two:

```css
.container {
    text-align: center;   /* centers the image */
}
img {
    display: inline-block;   /* optional – img is already inline */
}
```

---

## 6. Centering text inside a flex item

When a flex container has multiple items and you only want the *text* inside one of those items centered, you can combine both techniques:

```css
.parent {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
}
.child {
    flex: 1;               /* each child takes equal space */
    text-align: center;    /* center the text inside each child */
}
```

---

## 7. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| Text isn’t centered even though `text-align: center;` is set | The element is **inline** (e.g., `<span>`) and its parent isn’t a block container. | Apply `display: block;` (or `display: inline-block;`) to the parent, or set `text-align` on the nearest block ancestor. |
| Vertical centering doesn’t work on a `<div>` with `height: auto;` | Flex/Grid need a defined height to know where the vertical middle is. | Give the container a height (`height: 100vh;`, `min-height: 300px;`, etc.) or let it fill its parent (`flex: 1`). |
| `margin: 0 auto;` doesn’t center a block element | The element has `width: 100%` (or no width set, which defaults to 100%). | Set an explicit width (or `max-width`) less than 100%. |
| Text overflows the container after centering | The container isn’t wide enough for the text. | Use `word-wrap: break-word;` or adjust the container’s width/padding. |

---

## 8. Handy cheat‑sheet

```css
